<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/advanced/interaction-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The earlier design lessons answered the question <em>how should it look?</em> This one answers
    the harder question underneath it: <em>how should it behave?</em> Interaction design is design
    across time. A poster is judged in one glance; an interface is judged over minutes, as a user
    pokes it, errs, waits, recovers and succeeds. That adds a dimension static design does not
    have: the product has to respond, and the quality of those responses — their speed, clarity
    and tone — <em>is</em> the product as far as the user is concerned. This chapter works through
    the full design cycle as it applies to behaviour, then the four load-bearing concepts:
    states, affordances, flows and motion.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="states, signifiers, flows, meaningful motion" widget="equationStep" config={ixdCfg} />

  <h2>The design cycle, applied to behaviour</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus design process — identify, define, propose, develop, plan, test and evaluate —
    is usually taught on physical products, but every stage has a distinct interaction flavour.
    <strong>Identifying:</strong> needfinding means watching real users, not asking them. People
    are unreliable narrators of their own behaviour; a five-user contextual observation where you
    watch someone fail to find a settings option teaches more than thirty survey answers about
    "ease of use". You are identifying <em>tasks and breakdowns</em>: where does the current way
    of doing things cost time, cause errors or force memorisation?
    <strong>Defining:</strong> the findings become requirements — and for interaction, the honest
    unit of requirement is the task, not the feature. "Let users change their email address" is a
    feature; "a locked-out user regains access in under two minutes without emailing support" is
    a behaviour requirement, and it can be tested. Good definitions name the user, the task, the
    context and a measurable success criterion.
    <strong>Proposing:</strong> interaction proposals are cheap and fast — paper flows, clickable
    wireframes, Wizard-of-Oz prototypes where a human fakes the intelligence behind the screen.
    The point is to test the <em>choreography</em> before any pixel polish: does the order of
    decisions make sense? Where does the flow fork, and does the user understand which branch
    they took?
    <strong>Developing and planning:</strong> here the interaction designer's main output is a
    component's <em>state matrix</em> and a flow map, both of which engineering can implement
    unambiguously. Planning means sequencing: which flows ship first, and what is the fallback
    when the ideal data or network is unavailable.
    <strong>Testing and evaluating:</strong> two complementary methods. Formative usability tests
    (five users, think-aloud, task-based) find the big comprehension failures early — Nielsen's
    classic result is that five users surface roughly 85% of severe usability problems, because
    users' difficulties overlap heavily. Summative evaluation compares finished designs on
    metrics: task time, error rate, completion rate, System Usability Scale score. The mistake to
    avoid is testing only whether the design <em>can</em> be used by a motivated expert; the
    syllabus question is whether it will be used correctly by a first-time, distracted human.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "testing comes at the end of the cycle." In interaction design,
    evaluation is how you learn what to design, so it runs at every stage — a paper flow tested
    with three colleagues on a Friday morning can kill a month of wrong development.
  </p>

  <h2>States: the real design surface</h2>
  <p>
    The founding insight of interaction design is that a screen is not one thing but a machine
    with many configurations, and users spend most of their time not in the happy default. Take
    the humblest component, a "Save" button, and enumerate its states: <strong>default</strong>
    (looks pressable), <strong>hover</strong> (confirms pointer is over the target), <strong>focus</strong>
    (a visible ring for keyboard users — not optional decoration but the only way a non-mouse user
    knows where they are), <strong>active</strong> (pressed feedback, within about 100 ms so the
    response feels physically connected), <strong>loading</strong> (after the click: work is
    happening, and this state's duration determines whether you need it at all), <strong>success</strong>
    ("saved" — close the loop the click opened), <strong>error</strong> (save failed, say why, and
    crucially preserve the user's input), and <strong>disabled</strong> (unavailable — and why?
    A disabled state with no explanation is a dead end; good practice keeps the button enabled and
    explains on click what is missing). Designing the default state alone and shipping it is the
    single most common quality gap in real products. Empty states deserve special respect: an
    empty inbox that says "no items" is a dead end, while one that explains what will appear here
    and offers the first action turns the product's emptiest moment into onboarding.
  </p>

  <h2>Affordances and signifiers</h2>
  <p>
    Norman's distinction is small and endlessly misapplied. An <strong>affordance</strong> is a
    real possibility for action: a flat plate affords pushing, a handle affords pulling. A
    <strong>signifier</strong> is the visible cue that communicates where the affordance is. The
    distinction matters because digital objects have <em>chosen</em> affordances — a rectangle on
    screen can afford anything — so the entire question is whether the signifier is honest. The
    flat-design wave of the 2010s demonstrates the failure mode: designers stripped borders,
    shadows and underlines in pursuit of minimalism, and text that looked like a label became
    clickable, with measured increases in user hesitation and missed targets. The correction
    (material design's shadows, the return of underlined links and clearly bordered buttons) was
    not an aesthetic round-trip but a rediscovery that signifiers carry the interaction. The
    practical rule: every interactive element should answer "what are you and what happens if I
    touch you?" <em>before</em> it is touched.
  </p>

  <h2>Flows, not screens</h2>
  <p>
    Users do not experience screens; they experience sequences, and the sequence is where
    interaction design is won or lost. A well-designed flow has four properties. It makes the
    <strong>decision order</strong> sensible — questions asked in the user's order of certainty
    (destination before seat selection, recipient before message) rather than the system's order
    of convenience. It <strong>branches explicitly</strong> — every decision point has a designed
    path for each outcome, including "neither". It <strong>loops back on error without losing
    work</strong> — the user returns to the step that failed with their input intact and a
    specific message about what to fix. And it <strong>closes</strong> — a confirmation that
    states what happened and what happens next, so the task has a psychological end.
    A worked example: a checkout where the card is declined. Bad flow: a generic "payment failed"
    page that wipes the basket, forcing re-entry of address and card. Good flow: return to the
    payment step, pre-fill everything, display the bank's actual decline reason (expired card,
    insufficient funds), highlight the field to fix, and keep the basket one tap away. The
    difference is not cosmetic — abandoned carts are measured in double-digit percentages, and
    hostile error recovery is a leading cause.
  </p>

  <h2>Motion with meaning</h2>
  <p>
    Animation in interfaces is information, not garnish, and it does four jobs. It
    <strong>explains change</strong>: a panel that slides out from the button that summoned it
    answers "where did that come from?" without words. It <strong>shows relationships</strong>:
    shared-element transitions (the thumbnail that grows into the full image) teach the system's
    spatial model. It <strong>confirms action</strong>: a button that depresses on touch closes
    the feedback loop within the ~100 ms window where a response feels physically connected to
    the press. It <strong>manages attention</strong>: a spinner or skeleton screen signals that
    progress exists. The dosage rules are well established: UI transitions belong in the
    200–300 ms range (fast enough not to feel sluggish, slow enough to be perceived — below
    roughly 100 ms motion is invisible, above about 500 ms it reads as lag), with easing curves
    that start and end at rest rather than linear motion that reads mechanical. Motion as pure
    decoration violates the visual design lesson's clutter principle with a clock attached: it
    spends the user's time to say nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Latency is interaction design</h2>
  <p>
    The classic response-time thresholds (Miller 1968, reaffirmed by Nielsen) still govern:
    under 100 ms feels instantaneous; about 1 second is the limit for a thought to stay unbroken;
    about 10 seconds is the limit of attention for a single wait, after which users switch away.
    These numbers make performance a design property, not merely an engineering one. Three design
    responses follow. First, <strong>perceived speed</strong>: skeletons, optimistic updates and
    progressive loading (render the text, then the images) can make a page that finishes in the
    same total time feel twice as fast, because interaction is possible early. Second,
    <strong>budgeting</strong>: like the systems lesson's budget, allocate the response-time
    budget across components before building, so no feature silently spends it all. Third,
    <strong>graceful degradation</strong>: design what the user sees at each latency band, so a
    slow day degrades to "slower" rather than "broken".
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fitts's law quantifies target design.</strong> Movement time to a target of width W
    at distance D follows MT = a + b·log<sub>2</sub>(1 + D/W), with fitted human constants
    a ≈ 150 ms and b ≈ 150 ms per bit in common desktop settings. Worked example: a pointer
    moving 200 px to a 40 px-wide button has difficulty index log<sub>2</sub>(1 + 200/40) =
    log<sub>2</sub>(6) ≈ 2.58 bits, so MT ≈ 150 + 150 × 2.58 ≈ 540 ms. Double the button's width
    to 80 px and the index falls to log<sub>2</sub>(3.5) ≈ 1.81, MT ≈ 420 ms — a 22% saving for a
    purely geometric decision. The law's corollaries explain real conventions: screen corners and
    edges are infinitely deep targets (W → ∞), which is why menus anchored to corners are fast;
    and a command placed 600 px from where the cursor just landed costs roughly 150 + 150 ×
    log<sub>2</sub>(1 + 15) ≈ 750 ms every single time — layout decisions are repeated time
    taxes. <strong>Hick's law</strong> adds the choice side: decision time grows roughly linearly
    in the entropy of the choice set, so progressive disclosure is not just visual hygiene, it
    directly buys back decision time by presenting 3 salient options instead of 15.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Undo versus confirmation is a measurable trade-off.</strong> A confirmation dialog
    taxes every occurrence of the action to intercept the rare error: if a delete happens 100
    times a day and the dialog costs 2 seconds to dismiss, that is 200 wasted seconds per day to
    prevent a fraction of mistakes it does not even reliably prevent (habituated users click
    through reflexively — the "dialog blindness" every usability test finds). Undo inverts the
    economics: the common path pays nothing, the error path pays only when an error happens, and
    it pays in a recoverable currency. Gmail's undo-send is the canonical resolution. The
    boundary condition is actions that cannot be undone even in principle (sending to a
    thousand recipients, public publication with legal effect) — there, a deliberate-confirm
    pattern (type the object's name, show consequences) is justified, because the cost asymmetry
    is extreme.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Generative interfaces — systems where the UI is assembled at
    run time by a model in response to a natural-language request — are breaking the assumption
    that every state was designed in advance: what does a "designed state" even mean when the
    component is synthesised on the fly, and how are accessibility and error states guaranteed?
    Conversational systems also strain the classic feedback loop, since latency budgets differ
    (a 3-second wait before a considered answer is acceptable there but would kill a button).
    And adaptive interfaces raise a live research question: personalisation that predicts the
    user's next action can save Fitts-computed seconds, but prediction errors cost trust in ways
    the models do not yet price.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Norman, The Design of Everyday Things; Nielsen's 10 heuristics and response-time limits; Card, Moran and Newell, The Psychology of Human-Computer Interaction; the design, Fitts and form lessons.</p>
